# NEITHER REAL NOR UNREAL
## **The Inexpressible Appearance**
### *Yoga Vāsiṣṭha — Nirvāṇa Prakaraṇa, Sarga 195, Verses 44–47*

There comes a point in Vedānta where ordinary language begins to fail.

We ask:

### Is the world real?

If we answer **yes**, difficulty arises.

Everything we call “the world” is changing.

Bodies arise and perish.

Civilizations appear and disappear.

Experiences come and go.

Dreams arise and dissolve.

Even our understanding of ourselves changes.

How can the changing possess the same order of reality as the changeless?

But if we answer—

### “The world is absolutely unreal,”

another difficulty appears.

It is experienced.

Joy is experienced.

Pain is experienced.

Beauty is experienced.

Relationship, responsibility and consequence are experienced.

So the sages refuse both simplistic conclusions.

The world cannot finally be described as absolutely real.

Nor can it simply be dismissed as absolutely unreal.

They give us a remarkable word:

# **Anirvacanīya — Inexpressible, Indefinable**

---

## Consider the Dream

Last night you may have dreamed.

Within the dream, something happened.

Perhaps you travelled.

Perhaps someone spoke to you.

Perhaps you were frightened.

Then morning came.

Where did the dream go?

Was it real?

If it were absolutely real, awakening could not cancel its world.

Was it absolutely unreal?

Then how was it experienced?

So we arrive at an unusual territory:

### **It appeared—but not with independent reality.**

This is the doorway into *anirvacanīyatā*.

---

## What Does Knowledge Destroy?

This is crucial.

Knowledge does not necessarily destroy the appearance of the world.

The sage may still see a tree.

The body still requires food.

Someone suffering still deserves compassion.

Fire still burns.

Responsibilities remain responsibilities.

What disappears is subtler:

### **the conviction that the appearance exists independently of Brahman.**

This is why liberation is not the physical destruction of the universe.

It is the destruction of a misunderstanding.

---

## The End of the Seer-Seen Division

Ordinary experience contains a structure:

**I am here.  
The world is there.  
I am experiencing it.**

Experiencer.

Experience.

Experienced.

Three.

But if the innermost Self is Brahman—

and nothing exists independently of Brahman—

where can the second thing stand?

At the deepest level, even the statement—

### **“I experience Brahman”**

contains too much.

There is still an “I” here and Brahman there.

Therefore Brahman is not finally known as an object.

It is the very Reality because of which knowing is possible.

---

## You Cannot Stand Outside Yourself

The eye can see innumerable objects but cannot stand outside itself and look back upon itself in the same manner.

Likewise, Awareness illuminates every experience.

But when we attempt to make Awareness into one more object of experience, we have already turned toward another appearance.

Thought is known.

Silence is known.

Meditative peace is known.

Bliss is known.

The sense “I am witnessing” is also known.

Then the ancient question returns:

### **What knows even this?**

At this threshold, intellectual answers become less useful.

Silence becomes more truthful.

---

## When the World Loses Its Weight

This teaching is not merely metaphysical.

It transforms ordinary life.

Someone praises us.

An appearance arises.

Someone criticizes us.

Another appearance arises.

The body becomes comfortable.

An experience arises.

The body becomes uncomfortable.

Another experience arises.

Gain appears.

Loss appears.

Meeting appears.

Separation appears.

When each is taken as absolutely real and as defining “me,” life becomes unbearably heavy.

But when their dependent, passing nature is seen—

life does not become meaningless.

### It becomes lighter.

Compassion remains.

Responsibility remains.

Love remains.

But bondage begins to loosen.

---

## The Most Important Distinction

The destruction of appearances is not enlightenment.

### **The destruction of their imagined independent reality is wisdom.**

One need not erase the wave.

Know the water.

One need not destroy the ornament.

Recognize the gold.

One need not fight the dream.

Awaken.

And one need not destroy the world.

### **Recognize its substratum.**

---

## What Then Remains?

Not an empty void.

Not unconsciousness.

Not a blank philosophical abstraction.

What remains is that which was never absent:

### **Awareness itself.**

Before a thought—

Awareness.

During the thought—

Awareness.

After the thought—

Awareness.

Before the dream—

Awareness.

During the dream—

Awareness.

After the dream—

Awareness.

Before the question “Who am I?”—

That.

During the inquiry—

That.

When even the question becomes silent—

### **That.**

---

## A Quiet Contemplation

For a few moments, do not try to remove anything.

Let sounds be present.

Let sensations be present.

Let thoughts come.

Let thoughts go.

Now notice:

Everything that appears announces itself by changing.

But that which knows the change—

### does it itself appear as another changing object?

Do not rush to answer.

Rest with the question.

---

# Contemplative Essence

The world cannot finally be captured by saying—

**“It absolutely is.”**

Nor by saying—

**“It absolutely is not.”**

It appears.

It is experienced.

It changes.

Its independent reality disappears under knowledge.

Therefore the sage calls it—

### **Anirvacanīya — beyond final description as either real or unreal.**

And behind, within and as the very possibility of every appearance—

Brahman remains.

Not because everything else was physically destroyed.

But because the imagined second reality was never independently there.

### **The appearance changes.  
The Witness does not.**

### **The dream subsides.  
Awareness remains.**

### **Names and forms come and go.  
Brahman alone is.**

**Om Tat Sat.** 🙏🌺

